derivations.claim:
Let $f$ be nonconstant and let $D:=P_P$ be the total pole multiplicity attached to $P$ by step 2.2. By step 2.5, $\operatorname{Pol}(f)
e\varnothing$, so $D\ge1$. If $D=1$, then $\operatorname{Pol}(f)\cap P^\circ$ consists of a single point $p$ with $\operatorname{ord}^{\mathrm{pole}}_p(f)=1$, so the pole is simple and gives a principal part $c_{-1}(z-p)^{-1}$ with $c_{-1}
e0$; by, $\operatorname{Res}(f,p)=c_{-1}
e0$. The residue sum of step 3.2 then equals this single nonzero residue, contradicting step 3.2. Hence $D\ge2$: counted with multiplicity, $f$ has at least two poles.

